% COORDINATION_FUNCTION: Provides a shared metaphysical framework—human dignity as ontological gift, knowable through faith and reason—to coordinate AI development across diverse institutions. Solves the problem: 'On what grounds do we constrain AI systems, and who has authority to interpret those grounds?' The Magisterium offers a centuries-long tradition, institutional continuity, and a non-instrumentalist anthropology as alternative to technocratic or market-driven frames.
% TRANSFER_FUNCTION: Moves governance authority from purely secular/technical/market actors to Catholic institutional interpreters. Transfers legitimacy claims from technology vendors and governments to the Magisterium and Catholic institutions. Redirects resources (research priorities, institutional autonomy, moral authority) away from enhancement and transhumanist projects toward care, solidarity, and relational protection. Makes technology developers treat human dignity as a non-negotiable constraint rather than an engineering variable.
% ABSENT_VOICES: Secular humanists, techno-optimists, indigenous and non-Christian traditions, atheists, and post-Christian societies. They would argue that privileging Catholic metaphysics forecloses genuine democratic deliberation and imposes a sectarian framework on pluralist governance. They would demand that human dignity be defined through overlapping consensus across traditions, not through unilateral Magisterial interpretation. They are excluded by the integralist claim that the Church has unique authority.
% DISAPPEARANCE_RATIONALE: If the Magisterial claim to authority and the constraint it embodies disappeared, AI governance would bifurcate: secular democracies would default to human rights frameworks and market mechanisms (weaker protection for relational goods); Catholic institutions would lose institutional leverage; technology development would accelerate toward enhancement and augmentation without the friction Catholic moral teaching provides. Vulnerable populations protected by Catholic solidarity would be more exposed to pure market logic; families would face stronger technocratic pressure.
% FOUNDING_PROBLEM: Technology development in the late 20th and early 21st centuries began to threaten foundational human goods—embodied dignity, relational identity, intergenerational transmission—without adequate moral frameworks to evaluate the threat. Secular liberalism reduced dignity to autonomy and rights; market logic reduced it to economic units; transhumanism sought to transcend embodied limits. The Church offered an alternative: dignity as relational, embodied, transcendent, grounded in creation itself.
% FOUNDING_PROBLEM_CORROBORATION: Catholic institutional and theological authorities affirm the founding problem remains live. Workers' organizations, families affected by labor displacement, and disability advocates (outside the Church) corroborate that technology governance lacks adequate protection for relational and embodied goods. Technocratic elites and secular humanists contest the diagnosis, arguing that human dignity is better protected through rights frameworks and that technology transcends rather than threatens embodied limits. Independent analyses of gig economy, algorithmic management, and bioethical research show concrete harms to relational goods, but interpret them differently (market failure vs. theological violation).
narrative_ontology:disappearance_verdict(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, world_rearranges).
narrative_ontology:founding_problem_status(human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading, contested).

